y $f(x)=x^2$ surjective?","options":["No, negative outputs are not attained.","No, $0$ is not attained.","Yes, because the codomain is $\\mathbb{R}$.","Yes, every nonnegative output is attained."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"72e7cda4c4dfccd928734e98","time_limit":25},{"id":"surjective_function_new_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x)=x+1$. Is $f$ surjective?","options":["Yes, for any $y\\in\\mathbb{R}$ choose $x=y-1$.","No, $0$ is not attained.","No, negative values are not attained.","Yes, but only for positive $y$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dc931b74ef21662dc9695131","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_surjective_function_76e13d49_curated20261026_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Consider the unique function $f\\colon\\varnothing\\to\\varnothing$. Which statement about surjectivity is correct?","options":["It is surjective, because no codomain element is missed.","It is not surjective, because its range is empty.","It cannot be a function, because its domain is empty.","It is not surjective, because surjectivity requires a nonempty codomain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3ce6df876522a53cb1f67341","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_surjective_function_76e13d49_curated20261026_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does surjectivity depend on the codomain?","options":["Because the codomain always equals the domain.","Because changing the codomain changes the formula of the function.","Because every element of the chosen codomain must be attained.","Because the range is ignored in surjectivity."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"53e0e510317a27fc85ae5e01","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_surjective_function_76e13d49_curated20261026_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which horizontal-line property is guaranteed by surjectivity of $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["Each line $y=c$ meets the graph at least once.","Each line $y=c$ meets the graph at most once.","Each line $y=c$ meets the graph at least twice.","Each line $y=c$ never meets the graph."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4ee45efce0e5151fb0e88aa8","time_limit":30},{"id":"surjective_function_new_curated20261026_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$, $f(x)=x^2$, which property fails: injectivity, surjectivity, or both?","options":["Both","Only injectivity","Only surjectivity","Neither"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5286a6d91e520518083b0293","time_limit":30},{"id":"surjective_function_new_curated20261026_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Is $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^x$ from $\\mathbb{R}$ to $\\mathbb{R}$ surjective?","options":["No; $\\mathrm{e}^x > 0$, so not all reals are attained.","Yes; every real number is attained","No; $\\mathrm{e}^x$ is not a function","Yes; only positive targets matter"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aaf2b51251b507afd679b4b1","time_limit":25},{"id":"surjective_function_new_curated20261026_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $a\\ne b$ and let $f\\colon\\{1,2\\}\\to\\{a,b\\}$ satisfy $f(1)=a$ and $f(2)=b$. Is $f$ surjective?","options":["No, $a$ is not attained.","No, $b$ is not attained.","Yes, both codomain elements are attained.","No, finite functions cannot be surjective."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"46bc8f109924d0b2fe0a6635","time_limit":30},{"id":"surjective_function_new_curated20261026_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon X\\to Y$ and $g\\colon Y\\to Z$ be surjective. Is $g\\circ f\\colon X\\to Z$ surjective?","options":["Yes","No, never","Only if $f=g$","Only if both are injective"],"answer_mode":"server","fe